---
import FeaturesLayout from '../../layouts/FeaturesLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<FeaturesLayout
  currentSlug="comments"
  title="Comments - JSDoc markdown comments in compodocx Angular docs"
  description="JSDoc-style comments with full markdown rendering - lists, tables, code blocks, links, headings - plus project-wide @link cross-references resolved at build."
>
  <p class="text-cdx-text-secondary text-lg">
    Use plain
    <a
      href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/jsdoc-supported-types.html"
      rel="noopener"
      target="_blank"
      class="text-cdx-primary underline underline-offset-2"
      >JSDoc-style block comments<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a
    >. Markdown is rendered in descriptions: lists, fenced code, tables, links, headings.
    Multi-paragraph descriptions are preserved.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="ts"
    code={`/**
 * Renders a card with optional selection state.
 *
 * Use \`as="link"\` for a navigation-only card.
 *
 * | Mode    | Use case                  |
 * |---------|---------------------------|
 * | article | static content card       |
 * | button  | clickable action card     |
 * | link    | navigation card           |
 */
@Component({ ... })
export class CardComponent { ... }`}
  />

  <p>
    The block above the class header becomes the entity description on the rendered page.
    Member-level comments (properties, methods, accessors, host listeners) render inline on each
    member card.
  </p>

  <h2>Fenced code in descriptions</h2>
  <p>
    Fenced code blocks in descriptions are syntax-highlighted with Shiki, picking the same theme
    pair your project is using. The default highlight language for description-level
    <code class="cdx-code">@example</code> blocks is HTML. Assert against identifier text rather than
    the language class when writing custom downstream tooling.
  </p>

  <h2>Inline cross-references</h2>
  <p>
    Reference another symbol with <code class="cdx-code">{'{@link}'}</code>. Resolution is
    project-wide and runs across additional pages too.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="ts"
    code={`/**
 * See {@link Button} for the button-only variant.
 *
 * @see {@link Card.Header}
 */`}
  />

  <h2>Additional pages</h2>
  <p>
    Markdown files outside the source tree can be wired in via a
    <code class="cdx-code">summary.json</code> manifest. A node&rsquo;s optional
    <code class="cdx-code">slug</code> keeps its URL stable when the wording changes, and
    <code class="cdx-code">aliases</code> keep old URLs working. Missing files surface a per-page warning
    rather than failing the build, and nodes deeper than five levels are skipped. Relative links between
    Markdown files point at the generated pages, and <code class="cdx-code">{'{@link}'}</code> works inside
    additional-page markdown identically to source comments.
  </p>

  <h2>Markdown layout hints</h2>
  <p>
    Two conventions change how compodocx lays out Markdown in additional pages, the README and the
    other root files, and feature READMEs. JSDoc comments and READMEs next to source files keep the
    plain layout. Both render as plain Markdown on GitHub and npm.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Lead-ins.</strong> A paragraph or list item that starts with a short bold label ending in
    a period or a colon gets the label in its own column once the page is wide enough; on narrow
    screens the label sits above the text. The label must come first, be plain text (no code or links
    inside the bold) and be at most 40 characters long.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="md"
    code={`**Parts.** The header stays fixed while the rest of the panel scrolls.

**Modality.** Sheet and fullscreen are both modal.

- **Focus.** Focus moves into the panel when it opens.
- **Close vectors.** Escape, a tap outside the panel and the back gesture close it.`}
  />

  <p>
    <strong>Inline lists.</strong> An HTML comment
    <code class="cdx-code">{'<!-- compodocx: inline -->'}</code> directly before a bullet list renders
    that list as a row of chips, for short items such as browser versions or tags. Only the list right
    after the comment is affected; numbered lists are left alone. Everything else gets one base style;
    compodocx does not guess a layout from the content of a list.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="md"
    code={`<!-- compodocx: inline -->
- Chrome 125+
- Firefox 131+
- Safari 18.4+`}
  />

  <h2>Provenance marker</h2>
  <p>
    Flag a markdown file as AI-assisted with an HTML-comment marker on its first line. Detected on
    additional pages and READMEs. Renders a localized chip and sentence above the content. An
    optional value (model name, date, or <code class="cdx-code">key=value</code> pairs) shows in the sentence.
    <code class="cdx-code">--hideAiGeneratedBadge</code> hides it. For the source-symbol equivalent see
    the <code class="cdx-code">@aiGenerated</code> JSDoc tag in the
    <a
      href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}guides/jsdoc-tags/#provenance`}
      class="text-cdx-primary underline-offset-2 hover:underline">JSDoc Tags guide</a
    >.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="md"
    code={`<!-- @aiGenerated -->
<!-- @aiGenerated model=example-model date=2026-05 -->
<!-- @aiGenerated 2026-05 -->

# Component guide

The marker must be the first line of the file.`}
  />
</FeaturesLayout>
